+      "Special attacks": "Pounce (Ex). A charge can end in a full attack plus two rake attacks.\nImproved Grab (Ex). After a bite hit against a smaller foe, attempt to grapple as a free action without a touch attack or opportunity attack. A success pulls the target into the lion’s space and establishes a hold. The lion may grapple normally or hold with only its bite at −20 on grapple checks while remaining free to threaten and use its other attacks. In later rounds, a successful grapple check deals bite damage. The lion’s entry also permits raking after a hold.\nRake (Ex). Each rake is melee +7 for 1d4 + 2 damage. The general Rake rule grants the two additional attacks against a grappled foe and says the lion must start its turn grappling. Rake bypasses the usual −4 natural-weapon penalty in a grapple. Pounce expressly includes its two rakes.",
+      "Special qualities": "Low-light vision. See twice as far as a human in dim light.\nScent (Ex). Detect normal odors within 30 ft., or 60 ft. upwind and 15 ft. downwind. Strong odors double those ranges and overpowering odors triple them. Finding the direction takes a move action. Within 5 ft., pinpoint the source.",
+      "Skills and feats": "Balance +7, Hide +3, Listen +5, Move Silently +11, Spot +5. Racial bonuses of +4 to Balance, Hide, and Move Silently are included. In tall grass or heavy undergrowth, the Hide racial bonus becomes +12.\nFeats: Alertness, Run.",
+      "Encounter ecology": "Warm plains. Solitary, pair, or pride of 6–10. Advancement 6–8 HD (Large). Level adjustment —. Male African lions are 5–8 ft. long and 330–550 lb. Females are somewhat smaller and use the same statistics.",
+      "Timing note": "The Lion entry permits a rake after winning its Improved Grab check but gives no timing. The general Rake rule requires starting the turn grappling. The charge from Pounce specifically includes the two rake attacks."
